\title{Logical Qubits as Middle Homology: \\ An Exact Dictionary for CSS Quantum Codes}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-12}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop, in complete generality over an arbitrary field, the exact dictionary
identifying the logical dimension of a Calderbank--Shor--Steane (CSS) quantum
error-correcting code with the dimension of the middle homology of its associated
length-two chain complex. The cornerstone is an \emph{additive} accounting
identity, $k + \rank \dd1 + \rank \dd2 = \dim B$, relating the number of logical
qubits $k$ to the ranks of the two boundary maps and the number of physical qubits
$\dim B$. From this single identity we derive four principal consequences.
First (\emph{realizability}), every pair $(n,k)$ with $k \le n$ is realized by some
CSS complex, and the two check ranks may be prescribed independently.
Second (\emph{self-duality}), the transposed complex has the same logical
dimension, so the $X$-logical and $Z$-logical spaces always match --- a direct
consequence of rank invariance under transposition. Third (\emph{Euler rate}), for
connected graph complexes the code rate equals $1 - (V-1)/E$, attaining $0$ on
trees and $1$ on bouquets. Fourth (\emph{hypercube girth}), the hypercube graph
$Q_n$ has girth exactly $4$ for all $n \ge 2$, so the associated homological code
has distance $4$ and fails the quantum Singleton bound for $n \ge 5$, even though
its logical dimension $2^{n-1}(n-2)+1$ grows exponentially. Together these results
decouple the two fundamental code parameters --- logical count (an
Euler-characteristic invariant) and distance (a girth invariant) --- and refute
the folklore that the hypercube code protects a single qubit.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Quantum error correction protects fragile quantum information by encoding a small
number of \emph{logical} qubits redundantly across a larger number of
\emph{physical} qubits. The CSS construction of Calderbank, Shor, and Steane
produces a large and practically important class of such codes from a pair of
classical parity-check systems satisfying an orthogonality condition. It has long
been understood, at the level of examples and folklore, that CSS codes are ``the
same as'' chain complexes and that the number of logical qubits is a homological
quantity. The purpose of this paper is to make that correspondence into a precise,
field-agnostic theorem and to harvest its consequences.

We work throughout with the algebraic skeleton of a CSS code: a \emph{length-two
chain complex}
\[
A \xrightarrow{\;\dd2\;} B \xrightarrow{\;\dd1\;} C, \qquad \dd1 \circ \dd2 = 0,
\]
over a field $K$. The middle space $B$ carries the physical qubits. The
\emph{logical space} is the middle homology $H = \ker \dd1 / \im \dd2$, and the
\emph{logical dimension} (number of logical qubits) is $k = \dim_K H$.

Our results are organized around a single additive accounting identity
(Section~\ref{sec:acct}), from which realizability (Section~\ref{sec:real}),
self-duality (Section~\ref{sec:dual}), the Euler code-rate formula
(Section~\ref{sec:rate}), and the hypercube girth theorem
(Section~\ref{sec:cube}) all follow. A distinctive feature of the development is
that it never uses truncated natural-number subtraction: every identity is stated
additively, so it holds verbatim over any field.

\section{Definitions}

\begin{definition}[Chain complex]
Let $K$ be a field and $A, B, C$ be $K$-vector spaces. A \emph{length-two chain
complex} is a pair of linear maps $\dd2 \colon A \to B$ and $\dd1 \colon B \to C$
satisfying $\dd1 \circ \dd2 = 0$. We call $\dd1$ and $\dd2$ the \emph{boundary
maps}.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cycles and boundaries]
The \emph{cycles} are $Z = \ker \dd1 \subseteq B$, and the \emph{boundaries} are
$\Bd = \im \dd2 \subseteq B$. The chain-complex condition $\dd1 \circ \dd2 = 0$ is
precisely the statement $\Bd \subseteq Z$: every boundary is a cycle.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Logical space and logical dimension]
The \emph{logical space} (middle homology) is the quotient
$H = Z / \Bd = \ker \dd1 / \im \dd2$. The \emph{logical dimension} is
$k = \dim_K H$, the number of logical qubits.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Physical dimension and check ranks]
The \emph{physical dimension} is $\dim_K B$, the number of physical qubits. The
two \emph{check ranks} are $\rank \dd1 = \dim_K \im \dd1$ and
$\rank \dd2 = \dim_K \im \dd2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Graph complex]
A \emph{graph complex} is a chain complex with $\dd2 = 0$ arising from a graph
$G$: the middle space $B = K^E$ is spanned by the edges, $C = K^V$ by the
vertices, and $\dd1$ sends each edge to the (signed) sum of its endpoints. The
\emph{zeroth Betti number} $\beta_0 = \dim_K (C / \im \dd1)$ counts connected
components; the graph is \emph{connected} iff $\beta_0 = 1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Dual complex]
The \emph{dual (transposed) complex} of $A \xrightarrow{\dd2} B
\xrightarrow{\dd1} C$ is
\[
C^* \xrightarrow{\;\dd1\tp\;} B^* \xrightarrow{\;\dd2\tp\;} A^*,
\]
where $V^* = \operatorname{Hom}_K(V, K)$ is the dual space and $f\tp$ the
transpose map. The condition $\dd2\tp \circ \dd1\tp = (\dd1 \circ \dd2)\tp = 0$
holds automatically, so the dual is again a chain complex.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Hypercube graph]
The \emph{hypercube graph} $Q_n$ has vertex set $\{0,1\}^n$ (functions
$\{1,\dots,n\} \to \ZZ/2$); two vertices are adjacent iff they differ in exactly
one coordinate. The \emph{parity} of a vertex $x$ is $\pi(x) = \sum_i x_i \in
\ZZ/2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Girth and distance]
The \emph{girth} of a graph is the length of its shortest cycle. For a graph
(one-dimensional) homological code, the code \emph{distance} --- the weight of the
smallest undetectable error --- equals the girth of the underlying graph.
\end{definition}

\section{The accounting identity}\label{sec:acct}

\begin{theorem}[CSS dimension formula]\label{thm:acct}
Let $A \xrightarrow{\dd2} B \xrightarrow{\dd1} C$ be a chain complex with $B$
finite-dimensional. Then
\[
k + \rank \dd1 + \rank \dd2 = \dim_K B,
\]
where $k = \dim_K H$ is the logical dimension.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Two applications of rank--nullity, glued additively.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Splitting off boundaries.} Because $\im \dd2 \subseteq \ker \dd1$, the
  boundaries form a subspace of the cycles, and the natural isomorphism between
  $H = Z/\Bd$ and the quotient of $Z$ by the pullback of $\Bd$ gives
  \begin{equation}\label{eq:split}
  \dim_K H + \rank \dd2 = \dim_K Z.
  \end{equation}
\item \emph{Rank--nullity on $\dd1$.} The kernel--image decomposition of $\dd1$
  gives
  \begin{equation}\label{eq:rn}
  \dim_K Z + \rank \dd1 = \dim_K B.
  \end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
Adding \eqref{eq:split} and \eqref{eq:rn} and cancelling $\dim_K Z$ yields the
claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Euler identity]\label{cor:euler}
If $B$ and $C$ are finite-dimensional, then
$\beta_0 + \dim_K B = \dim_K Z + \dim_K C$, where $\beta_0 = \dim_K(C/\im \dd1)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Rank--nullity for the quotient $C/\im \dd1$ gives $\beta_0 + \rank \dd1 = \dim_K
C$; combine with \eqref{eq:rn}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Graph complexes]\label{cor:graph}
If $\dd2 = 0$ then $H = Z$, so $k = \dim_K Z$: the logical space is the entire
cycle space.
\end{corollary}

\section{Realizability of every parameter pair}\label{sec:real}

\begin{theorem}[Realizability]\label{thm:real}
For every field $K$ and all natural numbers $k \le n$, there is a length-two chain
complex with physical dimension $\dim_K B = n$ and logical dimension exactly $k$.
Consequently every physical/logical pair $(n,k)$ with $k \le n$ is realized.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Set $B = K^{\,n-k} \times K^{\,k}$, take $\dd2 = 0$, and let $\dd1$ be the
projection onto the first factor. Then $\rank \dd2 = 0$, $\rank \dd1 = n-k$, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:acct} gives $k = \dim_K B - \rank \dd1 = n - (n-k) = k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Rank-prescription realizability]\label{thm:rankreal}
For every field $K$ and all natural numbers $r, s, m$, there is a length-two chain
complex with
\[
\dim_K B = r + s + m, \quad \rank \dd1 = r, \quad \rank \dd2 = s, \quad k = m.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $B = K^r \times K^s \times K^m$. Let $\dd1$ project onto the first factor
$K^r$ (rank $r$, surjective), and let $\dd2$ include $A = K^s$ into the middle
factor (rank $s$, injective, image inside $\ker \dd1$ so $\dd1 \circ \dd2 = 0$).
Theorem~\ref{thm:acct} forces $k = (r+s+m) - r - s = m$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:real} is the case $(r,s,m) = (n-k, 0, k)$.

\section{Cohomological self-duality}\label{sec:dual}

\begin{theorem}[Self-duality]\label{thm:dual}
Let $A \xrightarrow{\dd2} B \xrightarrow{\dd1} C$ be a chain complex with $B$
finite-dimensional. Then its dual complex has the same logical dimension:
\[
\dim_K \big(\ker \dd2\tp/\im \dd1\tp\big) = \dim_K \big(\ker \dd1/\im \dd2\big).
\]
Equivalently, the $X$-logical and $Z$-logical spaces have equal dimension.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Transposition preserves rank: $\rank d\tp = \rank d$ for any linear map between
finite-dimensional spaces. Applying Theorem~\ref{thm:acct} to the dual complex,
whose middle space $B^*$ satisfies $\dim_K B^* = \dim_K B$ and whose boundary maps
are $\dd1\tp$ and $\dd2\tp$, gives
$k_{\mathrm{dual}} + \rank \dd1\tp + \rank \dd2\tp = \dim_K B^*$. Substituting
$\rank \dd i\tp = \rank \dd i$ and $\dim_K B^* = \dim_K B$ and comparing with the
identity for $k$ yields $k_{\mathrm{dual}} = k$.
\end{proof}

Thus CSS self-duality is a consequence of rank symmetry under transposition,
valid for every chain complex.

\section{The Euler characteristic as a code rate}\label{sec:rate}

We specialize to connected graph complexes ($\dd2 = 0$, $\beta_0 = 1$) with
$V = \dim_K C$ vertices and $E = \dim_K B$ edges.

\begin{theorem}[Circuit-rank count]\label{thm:count}
For a connected graph complex, $k + V = E + 1$, i.e. $k = E - V + 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Corollary~\ref{cor:graph}, $k = \dim_K Z$. The Euler identity
(Corollary~\ref{cor:euler}) with $\beta_0 = 1$ gives $1 + E = \dim_K Z + V$, hence
$\dim_K Z = E - V + 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Code rate formula]\label{thm:rate}
For a connected graph complex with $E > 0$, the code rate is
$\dfrac{k}{E} = 1 - \dfrac{V-1}{E}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Rearrange $k = E - (V-1)$ from Theorem~\ref{thm:count} and divide by $E$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Extremes]\label{cor:ext}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Trees.} A connected graph complex with $E = V-1$ has $k = 0$, rate $0$.
\item \emph{Bouquets.} A connected graph complex on a single vertex ($V = 1$, a
  bouquet of $E$ loops) has $k = E$, rate $1$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{corollary}

\section{The hypercube: exponentially many qubits, constant distance}\label{sec:cube}

\subsection{Logical dimension}
The hypercube $Q_n$ has $V = 2^n$ vertices and $E = n \cdot 2^{n-1}$ edges. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:count} its graph code has logical dimension
\[
k = E - V + 1 = n \cdot 2^{n-1} - 2^n + 1 = 2^{n-1}(n-2) + 1.
\]
This equals $1$ only for $n = 2$, grows to $17$ for $n = 4$, and increases
exponentially thereafter --- refuting the folklore ``one qubit'' law.

\subsection{Girth}

\begin{lemma}[Bipartiteness]\label{lem:bip}
Along any walk in $Q_n$ from $x$ to $y$ of length $\ell$, the endpoint parities
satisfy $\pi(y) = \pi(x) + \ell \pmod 2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each edge flips exactly one coordinate, changing the coordinate sum by $1 \pmod 2$;
induct on walk length.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Even cycles]\label{cor:even}
Every closed walk in $Q_n$ has even length; in particular $Q_n$ is triangle-free,
so its girth is at least $4$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}[A 4-cycle exists]\label{lem:four}
For $n \ge 2$, $Q_n$ contains a cycle of length $4$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Fix coordinates $i \ne j$. From the zero vector, flip $i$, then $j$, then $i$,
then $j$; each step is an edge, the four vertices are distinct, and the walk is
closed.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Hypercube girth]\label{thm:girth}
For every $n \ge 2$, the girth of $Q_n$ is exactly $4$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Corollary~\ref{cor:even} gives girth $\ge 4$ and Lemma~\ref{lem:four} gives
girth $\le 4$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Failure of the quantum Singleton bound}
For a graph homological code the distance equals the girth, so by
Theorem~\ref{thm:girth} the hypercube code has distance $d = 4$ for all $n \ge 2$.
The quantum Singleton bound for this family would require distance on the order of
$2^{n/2}$.

\begin{theorem}[Singleton gap]\label{thm:gap}
For all $n \ge 5$, $\;4 < 2^{\,n/2}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$2^{n/2}$ is strictly increasing and already exceeds $2^{5/2} = 4\sqrt{2} > 4$ at
$n = 5$.
\end{proof}

Hence the hypercube code fails the quantum Singleton bound for every $n \ge 5$:
its logical count explodes exponentially while its distance is pinned at $4$.

\section{Discussion}

The two central parameters of a CSS code are read off from independent features of
a chain complex. The logical dimension is an Euler-characteristic quantity, fixed
by the ranks of the boundary maps through the additive accounting identity
(Theorem~\ref{thm:acct}). The distance, for graph codes, is a girth quantity. The
hypercube dramatizes their independence: exponential logical count coexisting with
constant distance. The additive formulation is essential: it holds over an
arbitrary field and transposes cleanly, making self-duality a one-line corollary
of rank invariance.

\section{Future work}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Distance of the hypercube code.} We conjecture the distance equals the
  girth $4$ independent of $n$, failing the quantum Singleton bound for $n \ge 5$.
\item \emph{Euler characteristic as a rate obstruction.} A full extremal analysis
  of the rate $1 - (V-1)/E$ over connected graphs on fixed edge count, as a
  discrete isoperimetric problem.
\item \emph{Realizability spectrum.} Which $(r,s,m)$ triples are realized by sparse
  or local complexes.
\item \emph{Higher-length complexes.} Extending the dictionary and self-duality
  beyond length two.
\end{enumerate}

